    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d361">

                <pb facs="0469" n="387" sameAs="frus:frus1865p3/pg387"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Bigelow</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 77.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1865-03-23"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >March</hi> 23, 1865.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have received your despatch of March 6,
                    No. 44.</p>
                <p>Unemployed politicians can often raise vehement disputes upon mere surmises or
                    accidents, as they can sometimes prohibit all debate upon the most questionable
                    measures.</p>
                <pb facs="0470" n="388" xml:id="pg388"/>
                <p>The French government can very well understand that our delay in filling the
                    legation at Paris was involuntary, because that government has really found the
                    appointment of a minister here was a duty attended by some embarrassment.</p>
                <p>I thank you for the information you gave me concerning the negotiations of
                    Maximilian at Rome for a concordat.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Bigelow,</hi> Esq., <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
